.TH CLOG_SET_FIELD 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_field \- attach or update a persistent structured field
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

void clog_set_field(clog logger, const char *key, const char *value);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Attaches key with value as a persistent field, which then appears in
every later log line that this logger writes, between the func= and
msg= tokens:

  ts=... level=INFO proc=prog(pid):tname(tid) src=... func=... key=value msg=...

The library copies both key and value internally, so the caller can
free or change them after this call without the logger noticing.

A key that already exists gets the new value; otherwise the field set
takes a new field.

Every character of a key must be a printable US-ASCII character
(0x21-0x7e), and the characters '=', ']', '"' and '\e\e' are not
allowed.  The library silently ignores a key that is empty, or that
holds one of those forbidden characters, whitespace, an ASCII control
character, or any byte that is not ASCII.  It also ignores a key that
names one of the fixed tokens that every log line already carries (ts,
level, proc, src, func, msg, bt and bt_error), because such a key would
make a duplicate key= token in logfmt, a duplicate JSON key, or a
duplicate RFC 5424 SD-PARAM-NAME inside one SD-ELEMENT.

When a value holds a space, an '=', a '"', a '\e\e' or an ASCII control
character, the library double-quotes that value in the output and
escapes those characters with a backslash:
  - '"' -> \e"
  - '\e\e' -> \e\e
  - control chars -> \en, \er, \et, or \exNN

The exact syntax of an escape differs by output format (see
clog_set_format(3)): JSON uses \euNNNN Unicode escapes, and RFC 5424
syslog escapes a '"', a '\e\e' and a ']' as well as a control
character.
When the output format is CLOG_FMT_JSON or CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG and a value
holds text that is not well-formed UTF-8, the library replaces each
invalid byte with the Unicode replacement character (U+FFFD) instead of
embedding the byte raw, because JSON text must be valid Unicode and RFC
5424 requires an SD-PARAM-VALUE to be UTF-8.
An RFC 5424 SD-PARAM-NAME longer than 32 characters becomes its first
23 characters, then a '~', and then a hash of the full key in 8 hex
digits, instead of a bare prefix of 32 characters, so two different
long keys that share a prefix get different SD-PARAM-NAMEs.
In every format the library always neutralizes a control character,
including a literal newline and a carriage return, so a field value can
never split one record across several lines.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
key     Field name. Copied internally. Silently ignored if empty,
        invalid, or one of the reserved names listed above.
value   Field value. Copied internally. May be arbitrary text.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Thread-safe: protected by the per-logger mutex.
Every emitted line visits each attached field once; keep the field
count small if logging is a hot path.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_INVALID, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_remove_field (3),
.BR clog_clear_fields (3)
